Propagate dimensional limits through a signed assembly model (@engineering-interval-models: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/design/Tolerance-stacks-correlation-and-assembly-yield.lingua#r_3EFDXEEGHFYPJNVJNJEFH2Y6CN") { r_0E388D8E2EC1D967485C09A648
Reuse [Dimensions, tolerances, references, and an inspection question](../../inquiry/quantitative-literacy/practice/Dimensions-and-tolerances.lingua#r_0K38T1TBRR4N9ESP1FYR55H90T). A stated dimensional interval restricts which values are admissible in a model. It does not assign probabilities within that interval, describe a measuring instrument, or establish how frequently a factory reaches an endpoint. For this first calculation, assume every combination of the specified component intervals is admissible. This is a Cartesian-product set of possibilities, not a claim of statistical independence.

Let A=50±0.10 mm, B=20±0.05 mm and C=29±0.05 mm in the longitudinal tray model g=A−B−C. To minimize g, make A as small and B and C as large as allowed: 49.90−20.05−29.05=0.80 mm. To maximize it, reverse those choices: 50.10−19.95−28.95=1.20 mm. The nominal gap is 1.00 mm. All eight endpoint combinations lie between those bounds, and the bounding combinations are permitted under the declared assumptions. This proves the bounds are attained for this ideal input set, not merely conservative estimates.

More generally, for an affine quantity y=b+sum(a_i x_i), write x_i=c_i+e_i with |e_i|≤h_i. Its nominal value is b+sum(a_i c_i), and its deviation is sum(a_i e_i). The triangle inequality bounds its magnitude by sum(|a_i|h_i). If all combinations are allowed, choosing each e_i with the sign of a_i attains the upper bound and reversing the signs attains the lower. Zero coefficients contribute nothing. This derivation uses the shared signed-number and linear-combination preparation; no probability or calculus is required. Nonlinear models and restricted joint inputs require separate reasoning.

Suppose the teaching requirement is 0.90≤g≤1.10 mm, inclusive. The nominal baseline satisfies it, but its entire possible range does not. The range intersects the allowed interval, so some combinations can satisfy the requirement. These are different questions: whether a satisfactory combination exists, whether every allowed combination satisfies the requirement, and what fraction of production would satisfy it. Only the first two follow from the present possible-value model.

One candidate tightens the dimensional half-widths to 0.04, 0.02 and 0.02 mm without changing nominal lengths. The derived gap is then 0.92–1.08 mm, wholly within the teaching requirement. That establishes a conditional geometric guarantee if those input bounds and model assumptions hold; it does not establish manufacturing capability, affordability or measurement capability. A different candidate increases C to 29.40±0.05 mm while retaining the baseline other bounds. Its gap is 0.40–0.80 mm, disjoint from the requirement, so it is rejected even as an existence proposal under this model.

**Work:** enumerate the eight corners, then show algebraically why interior points cannot exceed them here. Compare a zero-width dimension and a sign reversal. Explain why shrinking a tolerance on a variable absent from the model cannot improve this particular result. Continue to [Tolerance stacks, correlation and assembly yield](../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/design/Tolerance-stacks-correlation-and-assembly-yield.lingua#r_3EFDXEEGHFYPJNVJNJEFH2Y6CN) for the existing assembly account; defer its covariance and yield calculations until the corresponding inquiry preparation. Root-sum-square is not a replacement for these hard dimensional bounds.

**Continue:** [Tolerance stacks, correlation and assembly yield](../mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/design/Tolerance-stacks-correlation-and-assembly-yield.lingua#r_3EFDXEEGHFYPJNVJNJEFH2Y6CN).

**Return:** [Engineering](../Engineering.lingua#r_5DA6VY09MAD2N8D9SH0FFJVV68).
